Q: How can we read request again and again as request stream is one time readable?

One line answer:
Original body is one-time; wrapper copies it once, then controller + validation can use it again.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Correct understanding (say this in interview)

1. Original request body stream = can be read only once
2. Wrapper reads it once and keeps a copy in cachedBody
3. Controller reads from the wrapper (not the original stream)
4. Because of the copy, wrapper body can be read again (multiple times)

So:
Original request  → one-time stream
Wrapper           → copies bytes once, then re-serves them
Controller        → reads from wrapper (re-readable)
ThreadLocal       → also keeps JSON string for validation business (if your design uses it)

In your filter:
filterChain.doFilter(wrapper, response)  // controller gets WRAPPER
Not the original request.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ True / False table

| Statement                                      | True? |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ----- |
| Controller reads from wrapper                  | Yes   |
| Wrapper got data from original request         | Yes (first read) |
| Original stream is one-time                    | Yes   |
| Wrapper is also one-time only                  | No — wrapper cache allows re-read |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Why is the original body one-time?

HttpServletRequest.getInputStream() / getReader() is a stream.
Once bytes are consumed, the stream is empty — second read gets nothing.

Problem without wrapper:
Filter reads body for logging / HMAC / custom validation
→ Controller @RequestBody tries to read again
→ empty body / binding fails

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Flow (draw this in interview)

Client JSON
    ↓
Original HttpServletRequest  (stream: read once)
    ↓
Filter creates Wrapper
    - reads original stream ONCE
    - stores bytes in cachedBody (byte[])
    - optionally puts JSON string in ThreadLocal for business validation
    ↓
filterChain.doFilter(wrapper, response)
    ↓
Controller / @RequestBody
    - calls wrapper.getInputStream()
    - wrapper returns NEW stream from cachedBody
    - can be read again safely

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ What does the wrapper do? (concept)

Custom CachedBodyHttpServletRequest (or similar) extends HttpServletRequestWrapper:

1) In constructor:
   - read all bytes from request.getInputStream()
   - store in private byte[] cachedBody

2) Override getInputStream() / getReader():
   - return a stream/reader built FROM cachedBody
   - each call can create a fresh stream over the same bytes

So: first read fills the cache; later reads do not touch the original stream.

Spring alternative (built-in):
ContentCachingRequestWrapper — same idea (cache after content is read).

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Example 1 — PROBLEM (no wrapper): second read fails

Imagine filter reads body for logging, then controller needs same body.

// BAD — filter consumes original stream
String body = new String(request.getInputStream().readAllBytes());
log.info("body = {}", body);

filterChain.doFilter(request, response);  // original request passed

// Later in controller:
// @RequestBody LoginRequest dto  → EMPTY / fails
// because InputStream was already consumed in filter

Dry-run:
Request body bytes: {"username":"afham"}
Filter reads stream → stream pointer at END
Controller reads stream → no bytes left → broken

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Example 2 — SOLUTION pieces (interview-friendly code)

Think of 4 small classes:
1) CachedBodyHttpServletRequest  → holds copy of body
2) CachedBodyServletInputStream  → re-opens stream from that copy
3) RequestBodyHolder (ThreadLocal) → optional JSON string for validation
4) CachedBodyFilter               → wrap + doFilter(wrapper) + clear ThreadLocal

────────────────────────────────────────
// 1) Wrapper — copies original stream ONCE, then re-serves

import jakarta.servlet.ServletInputStream;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequestWrapper;
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class CachedBodyHttpServletRequest extends HttpServletRequestWrapper {

    private final byte[] cachedBody; // the re-usable copy

    public CachedBodyHttpServletRequest(HttpServletRequest request) throws IOException {
        super(request);
        // ONLY place we touch the ORIGINAL one-time stream
        this.cachedBody = request.getInputStream().readAllBytes();
    }

    public String getCachedBodyAsString() {
        return new String(cachedBody, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
    }

    @Override
    public ServletInputStream getInputStream() {
        // every call creates a NEW stream over the SAME cached bytes
        return new CachedBodyServletInputStream(cachedBody);
    }

    @Override
    public BufferedReader getReader() {
        return new BufferedReader(
                new InputStreamReader(getInputStream(), StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
    }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
// 2) InputStream backed by byte[] (so it can be opened again)

import jakarta.servlet.ReadListener;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletInputStream;
import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;

public class CachedBodyServletInputStream extends ServletInputStream {

    private final ByteArrayInputStream buffer;

    public CachedBodyServletInputStream(byte[] cachedBody) {
        this.buffer = new ByteArrayInputStream(cachedBody);
    }

    @Override
    public int read() throws IOException {
        return buffer.read(); // -1 when finished
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isFinished() {
        return buffer.available() == 0;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isReady() {
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public void setReadListener(ReadListener readListener) {
        // not needed for simple sync example
    }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
// 3) Optional ThreadLocal — same JSON text for business validation on this thread

public class RequestBodyHolder {

    private static final ThreadLocal<String> BODY = new ThreadLocal<>();

    public static void set(String body) {
        BODY.set(body);
    }

    public static String get() {
        return BODY.get();
    }

    public static void clear() {
        BODY.remove(); // MUST call after request (thread pool reuse)
    }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
// 4) Filter — wrap request, store JSON, pass WRAPPER downstream

import jakarta.servlet.*;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.filter.OncePerRequestFilter;
import java.io.IOException;

@Component
public class CachedBodyFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(
            HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain filterChain) throws ServletException, IOException {

        // Step A: wrap → original stream read once into cachedBody
        CachedBodyHttpServletRequest wrapper = new CachedBodyHttpServletRequest(request);

        try {
            // Step B: optional — keep JSON string for validation / HMAC / logging
            String json = wrapper.getCachedBodyAsString();
            RequestBodyHolder.set(json);

            // Example business use inside filter (still does NOT break controller):
            // if (json.contains("\"password\":\"\"")) { ... reject ... }

            // Step C: IMPORTANT — pass WRAPPER, not original request
            filterChain.doFilter(wrapper, response);
        } finally {
            // Step D: always clear ThreadLocal
            RequestBodyHolder.clear();
        }
    }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
// 5) Controller — @RequestBody still works (reads from WRAPPER)

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class AuthController {

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public String login(@RequestBody LoginRequest dto) {
        // Spring reads body via wrapper.getInputStream()
        // which returns FRESH stream from cachedBody → works
        return "hello " + dto.getUsername();
    }
}

// Another place on SAME request thread can also use:
// String rawJson = RequestBodyHolder.get();

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Dry-run with real JSON

Client sends:
POST /api/login
{"username":"afham","password":"secret"}

1) Filter creates CachedBodyHttpServletRequest
   cachedBody = [123, 34, 117, ... ]  // bytes of the JSON (read original ONCE)

2) RequestBodyHolder.set("{\"username\":\"afham\",\"password\":\"secret\"}")

3) filterChain.doFilter(wrapper, response)

4) Controller @RequestBody:
   wrapper.getInputStream() → new ByteArrayInputStream(cachedBody)
   Jackson reads JSON → LoginRequest{username=afham, password=secret}

5) If something else calls wrapper.getInputStream() again:
   → ANOTHER new ByteArrayInputStream(cachedBody) → still works

6) finally → RequestBodyHolder.clear()

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Role of ThreadLocal (if used in your project)

Purpose: keep the JSON string available on the same request thread
for validation / HMAC / audit WITHOUT reading the stream again.

Rules:
- Set in filter after caching body
- Use only for that request’s thread
- Always clear in finally (avoid leak across pooled threads)

ThreadLocal is NOT the re-read mechanism.
The wrapper cache is. ThreadLocal is just convenient access to the same JSON text.

Quick compare:
| Need                         | Use                          |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Controller @RequestBody      | Wrapper (cachedBody stream)  |
| Filter/service wants raw JSON string | ThreadLocal (optional) |
| Re-read stream many times    | Wrapper only                 |

Order matters for ThreadLocal (important correction):
1. set(body)                 ← available
2. filterChain.doFilter(...) ← get() works HERE (controller + business)
3. finally → remove()        ← AFTER request finishes; then ThreadLocal is empty

During request → re-read from wrapper AND ThreadLocal.
After remove() → ThreadLocal body is gone (request already done).

Related Q → Can we use ThreadLocal body after remove and when should we clear it

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Interview Q&A

Q: Can the controller read the original request body after the filter already read it?
A: No. Original stream is consumed. Controller must get the WRAPPER.

Q: Is the wrapper also one-time?
A: No. Wrapper holds cachedBody and can re-open a stream from those bytes.

Q: Why doFilter(wrapper, response) not doFilter(request, response)?
A: So downstream (DispatcherServlet → Controller) sees the re-readable request.

Q: Filter vs Interceptor for reading body?
A: Body wrapping is usually done in a Filter (before Spring MVC fully binds @RequestBody).
   Interceptor preHandle often runs after the request is already being processed; for body cache, Filter is safer/common.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ 60-second answer (memorize)

“Servlet request body is an InputStream — readable once. If a filter consumes it, @RequestBody breaks.
We wrap the request, copy bytes into cachedBody once, then pass the wrapper down the filter chain.
Controller reads from the wrapper, which re-serves the cached bytes. ThreadLocal may hold the same JSON for validation on that thread, and must be cleared after the request.”
